NAME
    usb-update-menu.sh - non-destructive m-loader menu-only ESP synchronization

SYNOPSIS
    sh ./usb-update-menu.sh --esp MOUNTPOINT [--apply]

PURPOSE
    On a previously provisioned m-boot USB, update only the six external
    GRUB menu scripts loader/menu/boot-media* on the existing FAT32
    MBOOTESP. No GRUB rebuild, boot-sector image write, EFI executable
    replacement, filesystem format, ext4 operation or ISO copy is done.

REQUIREMENTS
    Linux; mounted actual MBOOTESP with LABEL=MBOOTESP, vfat filesystem,
    existing EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI, EFI/BOOT/BOOTAA64.EFI and external
    m/boot/loader/init. The ESP must be writable by the invoking user.
    Git checkout of current m-boot including the bundled GRUB 2.12
    grub-script-check. Host HOME outside the ESP, writable for backups.

    Safely stop QEMU and any USB consumers. Reidentify the physical
    USB disk by model/serial rather than assuming a /dev/sdX number.
    Verify all MBOOTDATA copies have completed and unmount ext4 before
    mounting the ESP for this operation.

OPERATION
    First run without --apply to validate source GRUB syntax and verify
    that the mounted destination belongs to m-boot; it makes no changes.

    --apply creates a private mboot-menu-backup.XXXXXXXX directory under
    HOME. It copies existing destination menu scripts there, then stages
    and verifies all six new files on the ESP before replacing only those
    scripts. The backup directory persists for operator-controlled rollback.
    Abort/partial failure does not silently erase backup or ext4 files.

EXAMPLE (REPLACE DEVICE WITH OPERATOR-VERIFIED MBOOTESP PARTITION)
    cd /m/src/git/m-boot
    git pull --ff-only
    lsblk -o NAME,PATH,MODEL,SERIAL,SIZE,FSTYPE,LABEL,MOUNTPOINTS
    sudo mkdir -p /mnt/mboot-esp
    sudo mount -t vfat -o uid="$(id -u)",gid="$(id -g)" \
      /dev/sda2 /mnt/mboot-esp

    sh ./usb-update-menu.sh --esp /mnt/mboot-esp
    sh ./usb-update-menu.sh --esp /mnt/mboot-esp --apply
    sudo umount /mnt/mboot-esp

    Do not run these commands if /dev/sda2 is not confirmed as MBOOTESP.
    Do not run usb-install.sh: it erases the whole physical device.
    For a partial failure, use the saved host backup to restore only
    the original ESP menu files before attempting a new boot test.

VALIDATION
    Successful script syntax and byte-comparison prove the copied GRUB
    sources; ISO boot and firmware input remain physical/QEMU tests.
    An already-flashed medium does not inherit Git repository menu edits
    automatically. This command closes that gap without reflashing.
